{"id":"neet-2026r-q077","pn":77,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Oxidation number","q":"The correct decreasing order of oxidation state of the underlined atom in each molecule is","o":["N̲₂O₅ > A̲l̲₂O₃ > H₂S̲","P̲b̲O₂ > N̲₂O₃ > S̲O₃","P̲₄O₆ > C̲l̲₂O₇ > A̲l̲H₃","P̲₄O₁₀ > S̲O₃ > H₂O̲"],"c":0,"s":"Underlined atoms: option 1 N, Al, S; option 2 Pb, N, S; option 3 P, Cl, Al; option 4 P, S, O.\nP₄O₆: P = +3, Cl₂O₇: Cl = +7, AlH₃: Al = +3.\nP₄O₁₀: P = +5, SO₃: S = +6, H₂O: O = −2.\nN₂O₅: N = +5, Al₂O₃: Al = +3, H₂S: S = −2.\nPbO₂: Pb = +4, N₂O₃: N = +3, SO₃: S = +6.\nOnly option 1 (+5 > +3 > −2) is in decreasing order.\nNote: the underlining is hard to see in the printed options; the underlined atoms are taken from the publisher's solution."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q055","pn":55,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Oxidation number","q":"Consider the following compounds :\nKO₂, H₂O₂ and H₂SO₄ (the underlined elements are K in KO₂, O in H₂O₂ and S in H₂SO₄)\nThe oxidation state of the underlined elements in them are, respectively,","o":["+1, −1, and +6","+2, −2, and +6","+1, −2, and +4","+4, −4, and +6"],"c":0,"s":"KO₂: an alkali metal always shows +1, so K is +1.\nH₂O₂: oxygen is −1 (each H is +1 in H–O–O–H).\nH₂SO₄: sulphur is +6.","fig":{"w":210,"h":30,"alt":"KO₂, H₂O₂ and H₂SO₄ with K, the O of H₂O₂ and the S of H₂SO₄ underlined.","d":[["t",4,18,"K",""],["t",13,18,"O₂,",""],["l",4,22,12,22,""],["t",64,18,"H₂","e"],["t",64,18,"O",""],["t",73,18,"₂",""],["l",64,22,73,22,""],["t",100,18,"and","m"],["t",145,18,"H₂","e"],["t",145,18,"S",""],["t",153,18,"O₄",""],["l",145,22,153,22,""]]},"sfig":{"w":220,"h":194,"alt":"H–O–O–H with +1 on each H and −1 on the oxygen; H₂SO₄ with S (+6) double bonded to two O (−2) and single bonded to two OH groups (O −1, H +1).","d":[["t",4,34,"H₂O₂",""],["t",42,34,"→",""],["t",66,14,"+1","s"],["t",66,32,"H",""],["l",76,28,94,36,""],["t",101,41,"O","m"],["l",108,37,128,37,""],["t",135,41,"O","m"],["t",141,31,"−","s"],["l",142,41,158,50,""],["t",164,58,"H","m"],["t",84,54,"−1","s"],["t",164,71,"+1","m s"],["t",4,142,"H₂SO₄",""],["t",46,142,"→",""],["c",140,128,10,""],["t",140,132,"S","m"],["t",118,120,"+6","s"],["l",142,118,142,94,""],["l",138,118,138,94,""],["t",140,90,"O","m"],["t",147,82,"−2","s"],["l",138,138,138,162,""],["l",142,138,142,162,""],["t",140,175,"O","m"],["t",140,187,"−2","m s"],["l",131,133,114,146,""],["t",108,152,"O","m"],["t",96,138,"−1","s"],["l",102,154,88,164,""],["t",82,172,"H","m"],["l",149,133,166,146,""],["t",172,152,"O","m"],["t",170,138,"−1","s"],["l",178,154,192,164,""],["t",198,172,"H","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q052","pn":52,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Identifying redox reactions","q":"Which reaction is NOT a redox reaction?","o":["H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl","BaCl₂ + Na₂SO₄ → BaSO₄ + 2NaCl","Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu","2KClO₃ + I₂ → 2KIO₃ + Cl₂"],"c":1,"s":"(1) H goes 0 → +1 and Cl goes 0 → −1. This is a redox reaction.\n(2) Ba stays +2, Cl stays −1, Na stays +1, SO₄ stays −2. No oxidation state changes, so it is not a redox reaction.\n(3) Zn goes 0 → +2 and Cu goes +2 → 0. This is a redox reaction.\n(4) I goes 0 → +5 (oxidation) and Cl goes +5 → 0 (reduction). This is a redox reaction."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q089","pn":89,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Balancing redox reactions","q":"On balancing the given redox reaction,\naCr₂O₇²⁻ + bSO₃²⁻(aq) + cH⁺(aq) → 2aCr³⁺(aq) + bSO₄²⁻(aq) + (c)/(2) H₂O(ℓ)\nthe coefficients a, b and c are found to be, respectively-","o":["8, 1, 3","1, 3, 8","3, 8, 1","1, 8, 3"],"c":1,"s":"Use the ion-electron method.\nReduction: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 6e⁻ → 2Cr³⁺.\nOxidation: SO₃²⁻ → SO₄²⁻ + 2e⁻, multiplied by 3.\nAdd: Cr₂O₇²⁻ + 3SO₃²⁻ → 2Cr³⁺ + 3SO₄²⁻.\nBalance O by adding 4H₂O on the right, then balance H by adding 8H⁺ on the left.\nCr₂O₇²⁻ + 3SO₃²⁻ + 8H⁺ → 2Cr³⁺ + 3SO₄²⁻ + 4H₂O.\nSo a = 1, b = 3, c = 8."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q078","pn":78,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Decomposition redox reaction","q":"Which of the following reactions is a decomposition redox reaction ?","o":["P₄(s) + 3OH⁻(aq) + 3H₂O(l) → PH₃(g) + 3H₂PO₂⁻(aq)","2Pb(NO₃)₂(s) → 2PbO(s) + 4NO₂(g) + O₂(g)","N₂(g) + O₂(g) → 2NO(g)","Cl₂(g) + 2OH⁻(aq) → ClO⁻(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) + 4H₂O(l)"],"c":1,"s":"A decomposition reaction has one reactant breaking into two or more products.\nLead nitrate breaks into PbO, NO₂ and O₂.\nNitrogen goes from +5 to +4 (reduced).\nOxygen goes from -2 to 0 (oxidised).\nSo it is a decomposition as well as a redox reaction.\nThe others are disproportionation or combination reactions."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q075","pn":75,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Displacement reactions","q":"Which of the following reactions is the metal displacement reaction? Choose the right option.","o":["2Pb(NO₃)₂ →(Δ) 2PbO + 4NO₂ + O₂↑","2KClO₃ →(Δ) 2KCl + 3O₂","Cr₂O₃ + 2Al →(Δ) Al₂O₃ + 2Cr","Fe + 2HCl → FeCl₂ + H₂↑"],"c":2,"s":"Reactions 1 and 2 are decomposition reactions (one substance splits).\nIn reaction 4 a metal displaces hydrogen, not a metal.\nIn reaction 3 Al displaces the metal Cr from its oxide.\nSo (3) is a metal displacement reaction."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q136","pn":136,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Oxidation number","q":"What is the change in oxidation number of carbon in the following reaction ? CH₄(g) + 4Cl₂(g) → CCl₄(l) + 4HCl(g)","o":["0 to + 4","– 4 to + 4","0 to – 4","+ 4 to + 4"],"c":1,"s":"In CH₄: C + 4(+1) = 0, so C = −4.\nIn CCl₄: C + 4(−1) = 0, so C = +4.\nThe change is −4 to +4."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q170","pn":170,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Redox Reactions","st":"Disproportionation","q":"Which of the following reactions are disproportionation reaction?\n(a) 2Cu⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Cu⁰\n(b) 3MnO₄²⁻ + 4H⁺ → 2MnO₄⁻ + MnO₂ + 2H₂O\n(c) 2KMnO₄ →(Δ) K₂MnO₄ + MnO₂ + O₂\n(d) 2MnO₄⁻ + 3Mn²⁺ + 2H₂O → 5MnO₂ + 4H⁺\nSelect the correct option from the following :","o":["(a), (c) and (d)","(a) and (d) only","(a) and (b) only","(a), (b) and (c)"],"c":2,"s":"In a disproportionation reaction one species is both oxidised and reduced.\n(a) Cu⁺ goes to Cu²⁺ (oxidised) and Cu⁰ (reduced): yes.\n(b) Mn(+6) goes to Mn(+7) and Mn(+4): yes.\n(c) Mn(+7) is reduced and oxygen is oxidised, two different elements: no.\n(d) Mn(+7) and Mn(+2) both go to Mn(+4), a comproportionation: no.\nSo (a) and (b) only."}
